<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-hydrostatic-balance" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Hydrostatic balance</title>

    <p>
        For an air parcel in <term>hydrostatic balance</term>
        (also called <term>hydrostatic equilibrium</term>),
        the upward-directed vertical pressure gradient force balances the downward-directed gravity force
        (<xref ref="ch7-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" />),
        and other forces can be neglected.
        Since the air parcel does not accelerate, the vertical equation of motion
        (<xref ref="eqn-motion-z" text="type-global" /> of Chapter 5)
        reduces to
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            0 = -\frac{1}{\rho}\frac{\partial p}{\partial z} - g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        This balance typically is rearranged
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" number="yes">
            \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} = -\rho g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where the negative sign indicates pressure decreases with increasing height in the atmosphere.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch7-hydrostatic-balance">
        <caption>
            An air parcel with edges parallel to the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-, and <m>z</m>-axes
            of the local Cartesian coordinate system.
            Since the air parcel is in hydrostatic balance,
            the gravity force <m>\vec{F}_g</m> points downward in the <m>-\hat{k}</m>-direction
            and the vertical component of the pressure gradient force <m>\vec{F}_{PG,z}</m> points upward in the <m>+\hat{k}</m>-direction
            with equal magnitude.
            Other forces acting on the air parcel in the vertical are neglected.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch7-hydrostatic-balance.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Rectangular prism with length, width, and height
                and vectors representing the gravity force pulling downward on the prism
                and the vertical pressure gradient force pushing upward on the prism with equal strength.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        When the equation of hydrostatic balance <xref ref="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" />
        is separated and integrated,
        it reveals that the pressure measured at some geometric height
        is the weight of the atmospheric column above a unit area of measurement at that height.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                - \partial p \amp = \rho g \, \partial z
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                - \int_{p(z)}^{p(\infty)} \partial p \amp = \int_z^{\infty} \rho g \, \partial z
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \int_{p(\infty)}^{p(z)} \partial p \amp = \int_z^{\infty} \rho g \, \partial z
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                p(z) - p(\infty) \amp = \int_z^{\infty} \rho g \, \partial z
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Since <m>p(\infty) = 0</m>, this produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            p(z) = \int_z^{\infty} \rho g \, \partial z
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>\displaystyle \int_z^{\infty} \rho g \, \partial z</m>
        is the weight of a column of air with horizontal cross-section of unit area above geometric height <m>z</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Likewise,
        separation and integration of the equation of hydrostatic balance <xref ref="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" />
        between geometric heights <m>z_1</m> and <m>z_2</m>,
        where <m>z_1 \lt z_2</m>,
        reveals the difference in pressure measured at two geometric heights
        is the weight of the atmospheric column with horizontal cross-section of unit area contained between those heights:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            p(z_1) - p(z_2) = \int_{z_1}^{z_2} \rho g \, \partial z
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>p(z_1) > p(z_2)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The equation of hydrostatic balance <xref ref="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" /> also could be written
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-hydrostatic-balance-geopotential-1" number="yes">
            \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} = -\rho \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial z}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        or even
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-hydrostatic-balance-geopotential-2" number="yes">
            \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial p} = -\frac{1}{\rho}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        using the definition of geopotential <m>\Phi</m> presented in <xref ref="ch4-section-geopotential" text="type-global" />.
    </p>

    <p>
        Note:
    </p>

    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The equation of hydrostatic balance <xref ref="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" />
                    is <term>diagnostic</term> (descriptive)
                    rather than <term>prognostic</term> (predictive)
                    like the vertical equation of motion <xref ref="eqn-motion-z" text="type-global" />
                    because it has no dependence on time <m>t</m>, and
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Vertical motion <m>w</m> can no longer be solved for using
                    the equation of hydrostatic balance <xref ref="eqn-hydrostatic-balance" text="type-global" />
                    since <m>w</m> does not appear in the equation.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
        Hydrostatic balance would apply perfectly to the atmosphere if the air was at rest.
        When we apply hydrostatic balance to a fluid in motion, we call it the <term>hydrostatic approximation</term>
        (emphasis on <em>approximation</em>)
        since it is no longer perfectly accurate.
        But under most atmospheric conditions,
        excepting those with very large vertical accelerations,
        the hydrostatic approximation is accurate.
        Consequently,
        we often approximate atmospheric pressure as the weight per unit area of the atmosphere above the height at which pressure is measured.
    </p>

</subsection>